  notes: |
    v4 — tempo perceived as 'laggy/slow' relative to Für Elise. Even
    rhythm (12 evenly-spaced notes) gave flat, plodding feel. Replaced
    with dotted-8th + 16th rhythm via Strudel *N notation:
      e5*3 d#5 = dotted-8th E + 16th D# (Für Elise signature figure)
    26-step pattern + .slow(2) = 4 sec/loop = ~95 BPM quarter (faster
    than Poco Moto's 80 BPM but with the rhythmic long-short figure
    that makes it recognizable). Bass aligned to 16th-step grid via
    *2 = 8th note duration.
- sha: e2a6209
  date: 2026-10-02
  kind: amendment
  files:
    - ui/src/domain/presets.ts
  notes: |
    v5 — *N was wrong: e5*3 = e5 e5 e5 (REPETITION = trill, same
    pitch repeated → blends into one long sustained note, which is
    exactly what user reported). Correct operator is @N (elongation):
    e5@3 d#5 = e5 for 3 parts + d#5 for 1 part of step = true
    dotted-8th + 16th. v4's "rhythm" was actually e e e d# (trill
    of same pitch) — that explains the "целые ноты ми" perception.

    Real score fetched from Wikipedia/Nohl first edition (LilyPond,
    public domain). Transcribed 8 measures of A-section (M1-M4 +
    repeat M5-M8), 3/8 time:
      M1: e5 d#5 e5 b4 d5 c5        (6 16ths)
      M2: a4 ~ c5 e5 a4              (a(8th)+rest+3 16ths)
      M3: b4 ~ e5 g#5 b4
      M4: c5 ~ e5 e6 d#5             (climax: e6/d#6 high register)
    42 events, .slow(4) → 8 sec/loop ≈ 90 BPM quarter. Bass:
    <a2 e2>*12 = 24 alternating 8th notes per loop, sine + lpf 400.
